\title{energese: ggplot2 Layers for H.T. Odum's Energy Systems Language}
\description{
Provides grammar-of-graphics ggplot2 layers for H.T. Odum's Energy
Systems Language (ESL) — also known as \strong{Energese}, \emph{Energy
Circuit Language}, or \emph{Generic Systems Symbols}. Odum developed
the language in the 1950s during tropical-forest studies at El
Verde, Puerto Rico (Odum & Pigeon 1970, funded by the U.S. Atomic
Energy Commission).
}
\details{
Every canonical symbol on Wikipedia's Energese reference chart
(\url{https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Energese.jpg}) is
exposed:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=geom_odum_source]{geom_odum_source()}}       — circle (renewable / external input)
\item \code{\link[=geom_odum_flow]{geom_odum_flow()}}         — bare arrow (generic flow)
\item \code{\link[=geom_odum_storage]{geom_odum_storage()}}      — Bertalanffy module
\item \code{\link[=geom_odum_consumer]{geom_odum_consumer()}}     — hexagon (consumer)
\item \code{\link[=geom_odum_interaction]{geom_odum_interaction()}}  — chevron (multiplicative junction)
\item \code{\link[=geom_odum_producer]{geom_odum_producer()}}     — bullet (autocatalytic producer)
\item \code{\link[=geom_odum_switch]{geom_odum_switch()}}       — bowtie (logic gate)
\item \code{\link[=geom_odum_self_limiter]{geom_odum_self_limiter()}} — semi-circle (saturating unit)
\item \code{\link[=geom_odum_heat_sink]{geom_odum_heat_sink()}}    — down-arrow + ground line (heat sink)
\item \code{\link[=geom_odum_transaction]{geom_odum_transaction()}}  — elongated diamond (money transaction)
}

See \code{\link[=plot_energese_reference]{plot_energese_reference()}} for a single function that
reproduces Wikipedia's canonical chart.

Two APIs are exposed:
\itemize{
\item \strong{High-level (recommended)} — the ggplot2 Geoms above, with
standard aesthetics. Vectorised over data rows; composable in a
\code{ggplot()} pipeline like any other ggplot2 layer.
\item \strong{Low-level} — vertex-tibble helpers (\link{odum_symbols}) that
return \verb{(x, y, id)} coordinates suitable for
\code{ggplot2::geom_polygon()}. Use these when you want to
hand-compose polygons, animate with \code{gganimate}, or render
outside ggplot2.
}
}
\section{Prior art}{

To the author's knowledge, \code{energese} is the first R (or Python)
grammar-of-graphics library for ESL. The closest peers are the
now-abandoned Java \code{EmSim} (an ODE integrator, no rendering API),
the TypeScript GUI editor
\url{https://github.com/sholtomaud/odum-energy-language} (Sholto
Maud, who also produced the Visio stencil that Wikipedia's
Energese chart is based on), and static SVG reference libraries
from the UF Center for Environmental Policy. See the vignette
(\code{vignette("energese")}) for a full worked example and prior-art
scan.
}

